maniT Three-Valued Logic Demo
=============================

================================================
  1. bool3 Literals and tif Branching
================================================
  bool3 values and their trit representations:
    True    -> trit: +
    Unknown -> trit: 0
    False   -> trit: -

  tif branching on bool3:
    tif True => branch +  (True)
    tif Unknown => branch 0  (Unknown)
    tif False => branch -  (False)

  Classical if/elif/else on bool3 (treating Unknown as not-True):
    True -> definitely true
    Unknown -> indeterminate — cannot decide
    False -> definitely false

================================================
  2. Ternary Operator Truth Tables
================================================
  tnot (negation: flip sign):
    tnot + = -   (True  -> False)
    tnot 0 = 0   (Unknown -> Unknown)
    tnot - = +   (False -> True)

  tand  (conjunction, min(a,b)):
       |   +      0      -  
  -----+---------------------
     +   |    +      0      -  
     0   |    0      0      -  
     -   |    -      -      -  

  tor   (disjunction, max(a,b)):
       |   +      0      -  
  -----+---------------------
     +   |    +      +      +  
     0   |    +      0      0  
     -   |    +      0      -  

  txor  (exclusive-or, (a+b) mod3 balanced):
       |   +      0      -  
  -----+---------------------
     +   |    0      +      +  
     0   |    +      0      +  
     -   |    +      +      0  

  Luk implication  (max(tnot a, b)):
       |   +      0      -  
  -----+---------------------
     +   |    +      0      -  
     0   |    +      0      0  
     -   |    +      +      +  

  Luk biconditional (tnot(a txor b)):
       |   +      0      -  
  -----+---------------------
     +   |    0      -      -  
     0   |    -      0      -  
     -   |    -      -      0  


================================================
  3. Three-Valued Logic Identities
================================================
  De Morgan (tnot(a tand b) == tnot(a) tor tnot(b)):
    Holds for all (a,b): true

  De Morgan (tnot(a tor b) == tnot(a) tand tnot(b)):
    Holds for all (a,b): true

  Double negation (tnot(tnot a) == a):
    Holds: true

  Law of excluded middle (a tor tnot(a)):
  (Not a tautology in 3-valued logic — can be Unknown!)
    a=+  =>  a tor tnot(a) = +
    a=0  =>  a tor tnot(a) = 0
    a=-  =>  a tor tnot(a) = +

  Idempotence: a tand a == a, a tor a == a
    Holds: true

  Commutativity: a tand b == b tand a, a tor b == b tor a
    Holds: true

  Distributivity: a tand (b tor c) == (a tand b) tor (a tand c)
    Holds: true

================================================
  4. Practical: Multi-Sensor Fusion with bool3
================================================
  Scenario 1: All sensors report OK (+)
    A=+ B=+ C=+  =>  vote=OK      

  Scenario 2: Two of three sensors report FAULT (-)
    A=+ B=- C=-  =>  vote=FAULT   

  Scenario 3: One OK, one Unknown, one Fault
    A=+ B=0 C=-  =>  vote=UNKNOWN 

  Scenario 4: All sensors return Unknown
    A=0 B=0 C=0  =>  vote=UNKNOWN 

  System safety: engine AND brakes must both be OK.
  (using tand for conservative AND — min of two sensor readings)
    engine=OK, brakes=Unknown  =>  safe=UNKNOWN 

  Emergency stop: trigger if engine OR brakes is FAULT.
  (using tor for conservative OR — max, but we check for fault=-)
    engine=OK, brakes=FAULT  =>  combined=FAULT   

================================================
  5. Epistemic Interpretation (Knowledge States)
================================================
  Assigning knowledge states to propositions:
    "The sky is blue"  ->  true
    "It will rain tomorrow"  ->  unknown
    "2 + 2 = 5"  ->  false
    "P = NP"  ->  unknown
    "maniT uses ternary logic"  ->  true

  Inference: combining knowledge claims with tand / tor
  Claim A (True) tand Claim B (Unknown):
    True tand Unknown = Unknown
    (cannot assert the conjunction when one part is uncertain)

  Claim C (True) tor Claim D (Unknown):
    True tor Unknown = True
    (can assert disjunction because one part is certainly true)

  Negation of Unknown is still Unknown:
    tnot Unknown = Unknown

================================================
  6. Triple Modular Redundancy (TMR) with trit_median
================================================
  Input value: 5

  No faults   A=+ B=+ C=+  voted=+
  One fault   A=+ B=- C=+  voted=+  (majority overrides fault)
  Two faults  A=+ B=- C=-  voted=-  (majority corrupted — wrong result)

  TMR can tolerate exactly 1 fault out of 3 modules.
  trit_median(a, b, c) is the native ternary majority vote.

================================================
  7. Three-Valued vs Two-Valued Logic
================================================
  In classical (Boolean) logic:
    AND truth table (2x2): 4 cells
    OR  truth table (2x2): 4 cells
    The law of excluded middle always holds: a OR NOT(a) = True

  In Kleene 3-valued logic:
    AND truth table (3x3): 9 cells
    OR  truth table (3x3): 9 cells
    The law of excluded middle does NOT always hold:
      Unknown tor tnot(Unknown) = Unknown tor Unknown = 0

  Practical differences:
    Binary: NULL in SQL is implicitly 'unknown' but SQL has
            three-valued semantics layered on top of binary.
    maniT:  Unknown is a first-class value; tif handles it
            directly without special-casing.

  Classical tautologies that break in 3-valued logic:
    a = Unknown:  a tor tnot(a) = 0   a tand tnot(a) = 0
    (Both are Unknown, not True/False as in classical logic)

Demo complete.
